Why: Windows refuses SetForegroundWindow to a helper without a foreground right even when attached to the front thread, so a launched app that took the front kept it, and a window shown without activation still opened on top of the user's window. Keep the launched app's new windows under the user's window, hand the front back by minimizing the launched window once (Windows then activates the user's window; the app is shown again behind it), and say plainly when Windows refused.

